<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aristotle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">384–<a href="/years/322-bce">322 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aristotle was a Greek philosopher of the fourth century BCE whose works covered logic, ethics, politics and natural science. His writings were translated into Arabic under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> and commented upon by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/averroes">Averroes</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a>, and they were preserved and studied in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. His Politics also contains a well-known discussion of the constitution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aristotle.jpg" alt="Aristotle" width="314" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: After Lysippos · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aristotle_Altemps_Inv8575.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The project preserved <b>Aristotle</b>, Euclid, Galen and Claudius Ptolemy for the medieval world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Averroes, working under Almohad patronage, produced commentaries on <b>Aristotle</b> that transformed European scholastic philosophy when transmitted through Latin translation, influencing Thomas Aquinas and the wider course of medieval Western thought.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its democracy, the most radical of the ancient world, became a reference point, admired or feared, for political thinkers from <b>Aristotle</b> to the eighteenth-century revolutionaries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The seasonal migration of tuna and bonito through the straits made fishing a major industry, and <b>Aristotle</b>, in the Politics, names Byzantium as a city where fishermen formed a large part of the common people.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Aristotle</b> moved to Macedonia, tutored the young Alexander and served Philip II as a diplomat.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-macedon">Kingdom of Macedon · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Full citizens formed a privileged class, while the land was worked by dependent rural populations, called by various names in Cretan sources, who were bound to the soil and excluded from political life; <b>Aristotle</b> compared them to the helots of Sparta, though he noted they were less rebellious.</blockquote><a href="/empire/knossos">Knossos · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Stageira, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in Chalcis, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.59,23.79&amp;year=-344" aria-label="Stageira on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5134 714 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Aristotle"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5584 984h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5584 984h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.59,23.79&amp;year=-344">What was here in 344 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Hence it is evident that a city is a natural production, and that man is naturally a political animal…&#8221;</p><footer>Politics, book 1 &#183; tr. William Ellis, 1776 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6762/pg6762-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…as it is not one swallow or one fine day that makes a spring, so it is not one day or a short time that makes a man blessed and happy.&#8221;</p><footer>Nicomachean Ethics, book 1 &#183; tr. D. P. Chase, 1847 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/8438/pg8438-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Poetry, therefore, is a more philosophical and a higher thing than history: for poetry tends to express the universal, history the particular.&#8221;</p><footer>Poetics &#183; tr. S. H. Butcher, 1895 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1974/pg1974-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…men come to be builders, for instance, by building; harp-players, by playing on the harp: exactly so, by doing just actions we come to be just; by doing the actions of self-mastery we come to be perfected in self-mastery; and by doing brave actions brave.&#8221;</p><footer>Nicomachean Ethics, book 2 &#183; tr. D. P. Chase, 1847 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/8438/pg8438-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…for as by the completion of it man is the most excellent of all living beings, so without law and justice he would be the worst of all, for nothing is so difficult to subdue as injustice in arms…&#8221;</p><footer>Politics, book 1 &#183; tr. William Ellis, 1776 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6762/pg6762-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…the roots of education were bitter, but the fruit sweet.&#8221;</p><footer>Diogenes Laertius, Lives of Eminent Philosophers, book 5 &#183; tr. C. D. Yonge, 1853 &#183; a saying attributed to him by Diogenes Laertius &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/57342/pg57342-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
